A typo'd cost alarm now tells you it's off instead of switching off silently. This is committed as 9470fd3d, and build, test (6,627 passed, 0 failed), clippy and fmt are all green.

The fix: if --cost-warn, YOYO_COST_WARN_USD or cost_warn_usd has a value that can't be a budget (abc, -1, 0, nan), yoyo prints one warning to stderr at startup. It names the source and the value and says what happens next. I ran the built binary in an empty temp dir and it printed:
```
warning: invalid --cost-warn value 'abc' (use a positive number of US dollars, e.g. 5.0); the cost warning is OFF for this run
```
The env-var and config doors print the same kind of line. All three appeared before the "No API key found" error, so the warning arrives before any money is spent.

Decisions, all written in the code comments and the commit:
• Warn, don't refuse to start. The cost budget only ever produces a warning and never stops a run, so refusing to start would be louder than the feature it guards. And since the line comes before any spend, you can stop at $0.
• `0` warns. Neither --help nor the docs say 0 means "off". Off is the default, and you get it by setting nothing.
• The message only says "OFF" when it really is off. A bad value on one source falls through to the next one. I found that --cost-warn abc with YOYO_COST_WARN_USD=5 still runs with a $5 budget, so "OFF" would have been false there. That case now says ignored; the $5.00 budget from a lower-priority source applies. Following the plan's wording exactly would have printed the wrong message, so I departed from it here.
• An empty `YOYO_COST_WARN_USD` counts as unset. VAR= yoyo is the normal shell way to clear a variable for one command, so it's a chosen "off". This also departs from the plan's "empty → warn" row. An empty flag or config value still warns.
• The raw value goes through sanitize_for_display and is capped at 64 bytes without splitting a character. parse_cost_threshold and its existing tests are unchanged.

Tests: three new tests cover the table of bad and good values per source, the fall-through cases, and escape bytes plus the length cap. For the positive control I made cost_threshold_warning always return None. Three tests went red, and after restoring the code all tests passed with no NEUTERED marker left.

Docs: cost_warn wasn't documented anywhere in docs/ before. I added a short "Spending warning" section to docs/src/features/cost-tracking.md.

Still open on #1030: max_auto_continues still drops a bad value silently.
